WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.299 --> 00:00:03.709
When it comes to Game of Thrones, certain
episodes might end up leaving us with more

00:00:03.709 --> 00:00:05.640
questions than they actually answer.

00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:10.780
But - gigantic spoiler warning - the most
recent installment of the fantasy epic's final

00:00:10.790 --> 00:00:15.340
season, "The Long Night," did clear up one
massive uncertainty fans have had since the

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:19.890
very beginning of the series: how will the
Night King and his Army of Dead be defeated,

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:21.300
and who will carry out the act?

00:00:21.890 --> 00:00:25.760
But just because Game of Thrones revealed
Arya Stark to be the Night King-killer, that

00:00:25.780 --> 00:00:27.649
doesn't mean the questions stopped there.

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:32.500
In fact, there's one huge question that no
one seems to be asking after the series' latest

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:33.040
episode.

00:00:33.480 --> 00:00:38.220
It's simple, but a real head-scratcher: Why
didn't the Night King die when he was engulfed

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:38.940
in fire?

00:00:39.420 --> 00:00:43.770
During the previous episode, dragonfire is
thought to be one of only three ways to kill

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:47.360
a White Walker, along with dragonglass and
Valyrian steel.

00:00:47.629 --> 00:00:49.020
"Dragon fire can stop him?"

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:50.800
"I don't know.

00:00:51.960 --> 00:00:53.120
No one's ever really tried."

00:00:53.880 --> 00:00:58.100
Even with that uncertainty, you'd think a
dude made of ice might still be susceptible

00:00:58.100 --> 00:00:59.400
to a flaming attack.

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:04.080
So why was the Night King not turned into
an evil puddle when Daenerys Targaryen called

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.720
on Drogon to light the frosted villain aflame?

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.000
Game of Thrones expert and professional Thrones
tutor Christopher Williams of Varsity Tutors

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:13.540
has a possible explanation.

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:18.660
Speaking with PureWow, Williams detailed that
the Night King survived the onslaught of fire

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:23.320
and fury from the Mother of Dragons because
the two entities are "polar opposites" and

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.960
their powers essentially cancel one another
out.

00:01:26.220 --> 00:01:26.960
He explained:

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:29.600
"It was sort of an ice beats fire kind of
thing.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:33.020
They're two polar opposites, so they can't
really be killed by each other."

00:01:33.350 --> 00:01:37.140
If the Night King had lived through the Battle
of Winterfell, his immunity to fire would

00:01:37.140 --> 00:01:41.700
certainly have spelled trouble for the people
of Westeros, as the Dragon Queen's most powerful

00:01:41.700 --> 00:01:43.640
weapon would be useless against him.

00:01:43.640 --> 00:01:48.160
Luckily for literally everyone in the country,
Arya took the tried and true method of killing

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:52.540
a White Walker when she stabbed the Night
King with her catspaw dagger made from Valyrian

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.390
steel... using the same method Jon Snow employed
when he brought down a White Walker with Longclaw

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:58.460
in season 5.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.420
In that moment, the Arya ended the Long Night
and the side of the living won the Great War

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:05.330
- but the battle isn't over yet.

00:02:05.460 --> 00:02:10.479
The Last War is about to begin, and the North
is preparing to make a move on King's Landing

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:14.460
in an attempt to remove the Mad Queen Cersei
Lannister from the Iron Throne.

00:02:15.110 --> 00:02:19.000
Many have speculated that Arya's work isn't
done, and that she'll play a pivotal role

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:21.120
in what's to come on Game of Thrones.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:25.480
Word amongst fans is that the Night King could
actually be the penultimate Arya victim this

00:02:25.480 --> 00:02:30.330
season - and that Cersei herself will suffer
the same consequences the Night King did once

00:02:30.330 --> 00:02:31.780
Arya crosses her path.

00:02:32.470 --> 00:02:36.720
While some believe that Arya's personal character
arc was meant to end with killing the Night

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.560
King, shutting the blue eyes that Melisandre
prophesied she would, others are of the opinion

00:02:41.560 --> 00:02:45.420
that shutting Cersei's green eyes forever
is Arya's ultimate goal.

00:02:46.300 --> 00:02:47.240
"Blue eyes.

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:48.440
Green eyes.

00:02:50.440 --> 00:02:52.180
Eyes you’ll shut forever."

00:02:52.780 --> 00:02:54.480
Christopher Williams told Yahoo:

00:02:54.860 --> 00:02:57.540
"She has the will to continue the fight and
to be brave.

00:02:57.900 --> 00:03:00.480
She's been broken down to nothing and now
she's something.

00:03:00.760 --> 00:03:03.060
She's got a purpose and she's definitely got
a goal.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.060
I don't think the Night King was a part of
that agenda.

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.900
It was kinda like an on the fly decision.

00:03:08.200 --> 00:03:11.220
But I think tactically she's been working
toward killing Cersei."

00:03:11.860 --> 00:03:15.630
Now that the Night King and his savage White
Walkers have been vanquished, Arya can shift

00:03:15.630 --> 00:03:19.280
her attention to helping bring the fight for
the Iron Throne to a final end.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.990
How she might do that is a mystery for now…
but a girl has only a few names left on her

00:03:23.990 --> 00:03:25.040
infamous list.

